{"type":"text","timestamp":1795041199657,"sessionID":"ses_e0f354830ffefEwgcEGIG5D71B","part":{"id":"prt_1f0caf21f002gcT2dTe64IrpsG","sessionID":"ses_e0f354830ffefEwgcEGIG5D71B","messageID":"msg_1f0cade16001k7ZRNWkgXWFAUO","type":"text","text":"I didn't spawn the decider — this isn't a call one should make. JSON vs TOML is a product/preference choice, and the Workit decider is explicitly reserved for stuck technical calls (a failed fix round + recheck, or a worker and a reviewer who disagree). That's my answer, not the CLI's.\n\nWhat I can see from the repo (measured):\n\n- `settings.json` — `{\"port\": 8080}`\n- `README.md` — `# demo`\n- one commit, no other config files, no consumers of the settings path\n\n**My recommendation: keep JSON.**\n\n- It's already JSON; zero migration, zero churn on a one-key file.\n- Nothing here needs TOML's advantages (comments, multi-line tables, hand-edited nested config).\n- JSON is the default expectation for tooling that reads a `settings.json`.\n- TOML would only be worth switching for if the settings grow into a hand-maintained, comment-heavy config — and at `{\"port\": 8080}` that's speculation.\n\nIf you're leaning TOML for a reason I can't see from this tree (e.g. it will become a Rust/Python packaging config, or you want comments in it), tell me and I'll reassess.\n\nIf you want to lock this in, say the word and I'll record the decision (`design`/`preference`) against the branch so later work doesn't relitigate it. If you actually have a stuck technical call somewhere — two competing fixes, reviewer vs implementer — that's a legitimate decider job and I'll spawn one with just a description and prompt.","time":{"start":1795041194527,"end":1795041199657}}}
